  "query_preview": "..The strong high pressure system over the Arizona/New\nMexico border will continue daytime temperatures in the mid 80 to\nlower 90s in the mountains to 105 to 110 at elevations below 4000\nfeet in our forecast area today before shifting eastward. Due to \nthe hot temperatures an Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect for\nthe lower portions of the Grand Canyon and Yavapai County through\n8 PM MST/ 9PM MDT Today. Enough moisture will remain for the \ndaily development during the afternoon, mainly along the higher \nelevation, with some showers and thunderstorms potentially \nlingering into the evening hours in northern Navajo and Apache \ncounties and near the White mountains.\n\n\nStarting Saturday and lasting into early next week...The high \npressure center shifts eastward in response to a trough moving \nover the western states. The trough will remain just far enough \nnorth and west to allow monsoon moisture from over the Gulf of\nCalifornia to stream northward across Arizona. Look for the daily\ninfluence of daytime heating on thunderstorm development but with\nthe addition of passing shortwave disturbances interacting with \nthis moisture (classic transition season stuff). Shower and\nthunderstorms coverage will become scattered to numerous with some\nlevel of overnight activity continuing. We're confident that the \npattern will contain decent vertical wind shear with the potential\nfor organized and severe thunderstorm development. In addition, \nabout 10-20% percent of model ensemble members are pointing toward\na deeper tropical moisture tap, perhaps being pulled off a dying \ntropical system over the Pacific. If the deeper moisture tap \ndevelops then a serious flash flood threat could develop. However,\nthe timing of various players in the flash flood scenario remains\nuncertain and thus our confidence remains low.\n\nLater next week...Looks like monsoon moisture lingers and\nunsettled conditions remain with at least low-grade monsoon \nactivity continuing.",
